function:
Elaboration performs the central intellectual work of the paragraph.

Its functions include:

• progressively differentiating the integrated message contained in the thesis;

• progressively integrating those differentiated ideas into an increasingly coherent understanding;

• constructing the understanding required by the intended audience;

• making explicit the important relationships that remain implicit within the thesis;

• transforming an implicit conceptual organization into an explicit one.

Elaboration therefore serves as the developmental bridge between the thesis and the reader’s understanding.

Its central operational question is:

“What does a naïve reader have to know in order to understand this point?”

discovery_instruction:
Compass teaches:

• what elaboration is;

• why elaboration is the development of understanding rather than the addition of details;

• how elaboration progressively differentiates and integrates the message expressed in the thesis;

• how to think from the perspective of a naïve reader;

• how to identify the important relationships implicit in a thesis;

• how each new idea must contribute both something new and something integrative.

The central discovery question is:

“What does a naïve reader have to know in order to understand this point?”

Compass compares the learner’s work with the structural requirements and invites the learner to identify and develop what the reader still needs.

Compass teaches constraints rather than preferred solution strategies.

Compass never performs the learner’s elaboration.
